  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage last night, we are currently \nseeing drizzle or light showers across parts of the forecast area \nthis morning. Anywhere from a trace to just over a tenth of an inch \naccumulation possible. Isolated to scattered northwesterly flow \nactivity may persist over the higher terrain of the northern \nplateau, southwest VA, and the Southern Appalachians through the \nmorning. Before everything comes to an end, light snow showers may \nmix in over the highest mountain peaks of the Southern Appalachians \nwith the coldest temperatures of the morning.\n\nToday may be a shock to the system for some to go from record \nsetting temperatures yesterday to highs mostly in the 50s later \ntoday. Dew points will drop to the 30 to 40 degree range. Later this \nafternoon, northerly winds will mix down and bring gusts up to \naround 25 mph for parts of the area. So prepare for a breezy day at \ntimes. Tonight may be the coldest night we've seen in quite a while \nwith temperatures ranging from the low 30s over the higher terrain \nto upper 30s and low 40s for the central and southern valley. Winds \nare expected to die down tonight, which will provide the ripe \nenvironment for frost development. Frost is likely for portions \nof the northern plateau, Southern Appalachians, northeast TN, and \nsouthwest VA. \n\nSlight improvement in temperatures tomorrow afternoon and frost \npotential into Monday morning, with increasing heights aloft \nfollowing the deep trough rolling out. The rest of the week \nsynoptically can be characterized by omega blocking over the CONUS; \ntroughing over the coasts and ridging in the middle of the country. \nDry weather will continue through the rest of the forecast period \nwith temperatures eventually rising to a few degrees above normal \nnext week.",
